\begin{frontmatter}

\title{An interpretable block-term tensor network with a separate clinical term for predicting incident atrial fibrillation from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG}

\author{[GAP: author names] [GAP: author order] [GAP: ORCIDs]\corref{cor1}}
\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author: [GAP: corresponding author]}
\affiliation{organization={[GAP: affiliations]}}

\begin{abstract}
Atrial fibrillation (AF) raises stroke risk. Electrocardiogram (ECG) models that predict its onset are explained only after training, by attribution methods that agree poorly. We asked whether a model whose ECG evidence passes through readable factors predicts incident AF from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG more accurately than a clinical model and a convolutional neural network (CNN). We also asked where in the cardiac cycle its evidence lies. We propose the block-term tensor network (BTTN), a Cox survival network on a frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor. Each block projects the tensor onto a frequency factor and a lead factor, and age, sex and ECG intervals enter a separate linear clinical term that bypasses these factors. Incident AF was defined from coded diagnoses in the MIMIC-IV hospital records linked to MIMIC-IV-ECG. Patients with an AF diagnosis, or an ECG read as AF, before the end of a 30-day blanking window after the ECG were excluded as prevalent or concurrent. At one year, BTTN with the clinical term had the highest area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of the methods compared, 0.7776 (95\% confidence interval [0.7664, 0.7888]). The clinical model reached 0.7597 [0.7488, 0.7713] and the CNN 0.7350 [0.7226, 0.7469]. The ECG term alone, the network evaluated without the clinical term, reached 0.7397 [0.7275, 0.7517], comparable to the CNN. The AUROCs of BTTN with the clinical term ranged from 0.723 to 0.745 at horizons from 120 days to 5 years after the ECG. These values were computed from one set of cross-validated survival risks, without refitting for any horizon. In the survival sample, which over-represents patients with AF, observed one-year AF rose from 0.55\% in the lowest risk decile to 11.4\% in the highest. Per unit of signal energy, beat-aligned occlusion changed the ECG term's risk most in the PR window. External validation outside this single health system is the next requirement.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
interpretable machine learning \sep tensor decomposition \sep deep survival analysis \sep MIMIC-IV \sep occlusion sensitivity \sep cardiovascular risk prediction
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Context and problem}

Atrial fibrillation (AF) is a common arrhythmia, and in people with asymptomatic AF the first presentation may be a stroke or heart failure \cite{Kashou_2021}. In the Framingham Heart Study, AF was associated with a near fivefold excess of stroke. The stroke risk attributable to AF rose from 1.5\% at ages 50 to 59 to 23.5\% at ages 80 to 89 \cite{Wolf_1991}. Over 50 years of the Framingham Heart Study, the age-adjusted prevalence of AF rose about fourfold and its incidence more than tripled, in part through more intensive surveillance \cite{Schnabel_2015}. Identifying the people who will develop AF could therefore guide monitoring before a first complication. Clinical risk scores such as CHARGE-AF estimate this risk from routine variables \cite{Alonso_2013}. These scores need the abstraction of clinical data, which takes time, whereas a model applied to a single ECG at the point of care needs no additional data abstraction \cite{Kashou_2021}. The resting 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) is thus a candidate source of risk information in its own right.

Deep networks trained on sinus-rhythm ECGs predict incident AF. A network trained on 1.6 million ECGs reached an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of 0.85 for incident AF within one year \cite{Raghunath_2021}. When an ECG network and CHARGE-AF were combined, discrimination was highest for the combination in an internal and two external test sets \cite{Khurshid_2022}. The ECG and the clinical risk factors therefore carry partly different information.

Reported performance also depends on how the outcome is defined. A systematic review of 14 studies found a median AUROC of 0.740, with higher values for 31-day confirmation windows than for 1-year windows (0.851 against 0.630) \cite{Tsiartas_2025}. Eleven of these studies cited possible misclassification of negative cases. A model of incident AF therefore has to be judged with its outcome window and its label source stated.

\subsection{Research gap}

The networks behind these results are black boxes, and their explanations are computed after training. A commentary on predicting incident AF in sinus rhythm is subtitled ``more than just trusting the `black box'\,'' \cite{Kashou_2021}, and a narrative review names interpretability as a barrier to clinical use \cite{Saiz_Vivo_2025}. The 2024 guideline of the European Society of Cardiology addresses the neighbouring task of automated AF diagnosis. It states that the clinical performance and broad applicability of such algorithms are not yet known \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. The guideline thus leaves open whether such algorithms perform in clinical use, and the narrative review counts their limited interpretability among the barriers to that use. The explanations themselves are uncertain. Twelve attribution methods applied to ECG classifiers agreed poorly and were only moderately consistent across training runs for most methods \cite{Arends_2026}. For some of these methods, randomising the weights of the network did not remove the attribution pattern entirely. An explanation of this kind may describe the attribution method as much as the model. Rudin argued that a model for high-stakes decisions should be interpretable by design, instead of a black box explained afterwards \cite{Rudin_2019}. For incident AF, such a model would have to keep the discrimination of a deep network while exposing what it reads from the ECG.

Block-term tensor models offer one route to such a model. A block-term decomposition splits a multiway signal into blocks with low-rank factors per mode \cite{De_Lathauwer_2008}. A mode is one axis of the multiway array, such as frequency, time or lead, and a low-rank factor is a matrix with few columns, so that the block describes its mode by a few components. In neural networks, block-term structure has compressed weight matrices, and those networks are explained with Grad-CAM maps instead of through their factors \cite{arxiv_2010_04963}. Grad-CAM weights the feature maps of a convolutional layer by the gradient of the output and so marks the input regions that most raise the output. In supervised regression of brain signals, block-term tensor regression, designed for interpretability, predicted finger movements \cite{Faes_2024}. That regression is linear and has no survival objective, which incident prediction with censored follow-up needs. To our knowledge, no published model places readable block-term frequency and lead factors on the predictive path of a survival network for incident AF. Such a model would also need a way to add clinical risk factors, which complement the ECG \cite{Khurshid_2022}. The clinical factors should not enter the factors that are meant to be read, or the reading would mix the two sources.

\subsection{Aim and contributions}

This study addresses two questions. The first is whether a block-term tensor network predicts incident AF from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG more accurately than a clinical model and a convolutional neural network (CNN). In this network, the ECG evidence passes through readable frequency and lead factors. Age, sex and ECG intervals enter through a separate linear term. The second question is where in the cardiac cycle the ECG evidence of the network lies. We answer both questions in MIMIC-IV-ECG, with incident AF defined from hospital diagnoses coded in the International Classification of Diseases (ICD). We did not use labels read from the ECG as outcomes, since a third of a sample of 700 AF-labelled ECGs of this database did not show AF on expert review (Section~\ref{sec:cohort}) \cite{Valasakis_2026}. All methods share one cohort, one set of folds and one set of risk factors. Differences between them are therefore differences of model.

The contributions of the study are these:
\begin{itemize}
\item BTTN, a survival network whose ECG evidence passes through per-block frequency and lead factors, with a separate linear clinical term that does not pass through those factors.
\item An incident-AF cohort in MIMIC-IV-ECG defined from ICD-coded hospital diagnoses, reported with its exclusion counts. The cohort excludes as prevalent or concurrent the patients with an AF diagnosis, or an ECG read as AF, before the end of a 30-day blanking window after the index ECG.
\item A comparison of one-year discrimination against a clinical model, a CNN and linear block-term tensor regression (BTTR) under one protocol.
\item The discrimination of BTTN at four horizons, read from the same cross-validated risks without refitting for each horizon.
\item The calibration, risk stratification and operating points of BTTN with the clinical term.
\item A beat-aligned, energy-normalised occlusion profile of the ECG term of BTTN.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related work}

\subsection{AI-ECG prediction of incident AF}

Two tasks are reported under the heading of AF prediction from the sinus-rhythm ECG, and they differ in what counts as a case. In identification, a case is a patient who already has AF or shows it soon after the ECG. A CNN developed on 649,931 sinus-rhythm ECGs of 180,922 Mayo Clinic patients, 454,789 of them in its training set, identified patients with AF with an AUROC of 0.87, counting AF before the tested ECG as positive \cite{Attia_2019}. A network for AF within 31 days of a sinus-rhythm ECG reached AUROCs of 0.86 on held-out Veterans Affairs ECGs and 0.93 at a non-VA site \cite{Yuan_2023}. In incident prediction, a case is a patient whose first AF follows the ECG after a stated interval. The two tasks give different numbers. In a systematic review, models with 31-day confirmation windows had higher median AUROCs than models with 1-year windows \cite{Tsiartas_2025}, and a narrative review found AI more accurate at shorter event horizons \cite{Saiz_Vivo_2025}. The present study addresses incident prediction only.

Studies of dated incident AF report lower values. Raghunath et al.\ reported an AUROC of 0.85 for incident AF within one year on ECGs from 430,000 patients \cite{Raghunath_2021}. For a model from a Montreal cardiac centre, the AUROC was 0.78 at five years and 0.77 on external validation in MIMIC-IV \cite{Jabbour_2024}. Its abstract does not state the label source used in MIMIC-IV. On the hospital from which MIMIC-IV comes, the C-index was 0.750 for a CNN trained with a discrete-time survival loss \cite{Sau_2026} and 0.754 for a model of ECG images \cite{Zeidaabadi_2026}. In Brazilian telehealth data, the data family of an earlier 12-lead diagnostic network \cite{Ribeiro_2020}, a residual CNN with survival analysis had an AUROC of 0.845 \cite{Habineza_2023}. With a mean follow-up of 80 days, a gradient-boosting survival model of 417 engineered ECG features gave a C-index of 0.781 \cite{Riek_2026}. These designs differ in horizon, population and label source, so their values do not rank the models.

The explanations in these studies are computed after training. Saliency mapping gave the P-wave the greatest influence in one ECG network \cite{Khurshid_2022}. Saliency of the image model focused on P-wave morphology and the PR interval \cite{Zeidaabadi_2026}. The feature-based model named lower P-wave amplitude among its predictors \cite{Riek_2026}. These reports agree on the atrial part of the beat. We found no test, in these reports, of whether the explanation reflects what the model uses to predict.

\subsection{Clinical risk models and their combination with the ECG}

Clinical risk models set the comparator that an ECG model has to match. The Framingham score predicted 10-year AF from age, sex, a heart murmur, heart failure, systolic blood pressure, hypertension treatment, body mass index and the PR interval, with a C-index of 0.78 \cite{Schnabel_2009}. CHARGE-AF reached 0.765 at derivation and 0.664 and 0.705 in two validation cohorts \cite{Alonso_2013}. Adding ECG variables did not improve its discrimination. ECG intervals measured in sinus rhythm are nevertheless AF predictors in their own right. In Framingham, each 20 ms increment of the PR interval carried an adjusted hazard ratio of 1.12 for AF \cite{Cheng_2009}. Across Framingham and ARIC, a P-wave duration above 120 ms was associated with AF (hazard ratio 1.55), but P-wave indices added little discrimination beyond traditional risk factors \cite{Magnani_2015}. These results motivate the ECG intervals in the clinical term of this study. They also set a modest expectation for what interval measurements alone add.

Studies that combined an ECG network with clinical factors found the two complementary in most cohorts. AUROC was comparable for CHARGE-AF and an ECG network in three test sets and highest for their combination, at 0.838, 0.777 and 0.746 \cite{Khurshid_2022}. In an older community cohort, an AI-ECG and CHARGE-AF had equal C-indices of 0.69, predicted AF independently, and reached 0.72 together \cite{Christopoulos_2020}. A single-lead ECG model with age and sex had an AUROC of 0.695 against 0.679 for CHARGE-AF in trial data \cite{Khurshid_2025}. A random forest that combined demographic, clinical, engineered and deep ECG features reached 0.909, against 0.839 for the best single modality \cite{Biton_2021}.

Other studies found the ECG model ahead of the clinical score. In 2,301 patients of a Montreal cohort, an ECG model exceeded CHARGE-AF, which reached an AUROC of 0.62, and an AF polygenic score, which reached 0.59 \cite{Jabbour_2024}. An image-based ECG model reached 0.696 against 0.667 for CHARGE-AF, and 0.711 when the two were combined \cite{Zeidaabadi_2026}. The ranking of ECG and clinical models also depends on the cohort. An ECG model led in hospital patients, while CHARGE-AF was the best single predictor in UK Biobank \cite{Sau_2026}. Fusing the two sources is therefore supported, but the way the fusion is built decides whether the ECG part can still be read.

\subsection{Interpretable models and post-hoc explanation}

Post-hoc explanations have been put to tests, and several methods fail them. Tests that randomise the model parameters and the training labels showed some saliency methods to be independent of both the model and the data-generating process \cite{arxiv_1810_03292}. Remove-and-retrain evaluation replaces the inputs that an attribution method ranks most important with an uninformative value, retrains the model and measures how far its accuracy falls \cite{arxiv_1806_10758}. Under this evaluation, only VarGrad and SmoothGrad-Squared, of the methods tested, did better than a random assignment of importance \cite{arxiv_1806_10758}. On the ECG, twelve attribution methods agreed poorly with one another \cite{Arends_2026}. Their self-consistency across initialisations was moderate for most methods, with mean correlations of 0.41 to 0.65. Dataset-level auditing with sanity checks gives a more favourable reading, in which aggregated saliency aligned with the decision rules of cardiologists \cite{Wagner_2024}. In that work the same methods also supported knowledge discovery, such as subtypes of myocardial infarction. The balance of this evidence leaves any explanation computed after training open to doubt about what it shows. It motivates models whose readable parts lie on the path of the prediction itself.

Such models route the prediction through parts that can be read. Concept bottleneck models first predict named concepts and then the label, at accuracy competitive with end-to-end models \cite{arxiv_2007_04612}. Correcting the predicted concepts at test time raised their accuracy, which shows one use of a readable intermediate layer. Their concept representations can, however, encode information beyond the named concepts, which makes the reading of the downstream prediction misleading \cite{arxiv_2106_13314}. B-cos networks summarise the full computation as one linear transform at similar ImageNet accuracy \cite{arxiv_2205_10268}. An anti-aliased variant gave explanations without artifacts on chest x-rays while keeping its predictive performance \cite{arxiv_2507_16761}. The multilinear structure of tensor networks has also been combined with deep networks, in preliminary results, to give weight-based interpretability at accuracy equal to baselines \cite{arxiv_2504_02667}. We call the encoding of information beyond the named concepts leakage. Leakage bears on any nonlinear head placed on readable factors, including the head of the model in this study.

Whether intrinsic interpretability costs accuracy on the ECG is contested. A study of variational-autoencoder representations of interpretable ECG features reported a cost of intrinsic explainability \cite{Patlatzoglou_2025}. A supervised block-term discriminant method reported none on EEG decoding, with a mean accuracy of 64.52\% against 61.00\% for its predecessor, higher-order discriminant analysis, on motor imagery \cite{arxiv_2511_04292}. Image networks that are interpretable by design also kept their accuracy \cite{arxiv_2205_10268,arxiv_2507_16761}. The answer may depend on the model family and on the task. None of the studies reviewed here compares an intrinsically interpretable model with a CNN on incident AF under one protocol. That comparison is one aim of the present work.

\subsection{Block-term tensor models}

The uses of block-term structure differ in where the factors sit: in the weights of a network, within a recording, or on the input of a predictive model. Each use rests on the block-term decomposition, which writes a tensor as a sum of blocks of low multilinear rank, that is, blocks that each need only a few components along each mode \cite{De_Lathauwer_2008}. It contains the CP and Tucker decompositions as special cases \cite{De_Lathauwer_2008}. The CP decomposition writes a tensor as a sum of rank-one tensors, and the Tucker decomposition is a higher-order form of principal component analysis, as a survey of both describes \cite{Kolda_2009}. Block-term tensor neural networks approximate the weight matrices of CNNs and recurrent networks by block-term tensors to compress them, and their authors explain the compressed networks with Grad-CAM \cite{arxiv_2010_04963}. The model of this paper shares part of that name. Its factors act on the input and can be read. On AF ECGs, a regularised block-term decomposition extracted several signals interpretable as AF patterns \cite{Goulart_2020}. That work separates sources within a recording and does not predict risk.

Supervised block-term models extract factors that both predict and can be read. Block-term tensor regression (BTTR) predicted finger trajectories from intracranial recordings, with automatic extraction of its components \cite{Faes_2024}. A block-term discriminant analysis gave interpretable and efficient dimensionality reduction for brain-computer interfaces, with an AUROC of 91.25\% on event-related potentials \cite{arxiv_2511_04292}. Linear BTTR has also been applied to the identification of AF in sinus rhythm on the median beat \cite{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22227432}, which is a different task from dated incidence. These models fit their blocks by deflation, which fits one block, subtracts its fit from the data and fits the next block to the remainder. They score linearly and have no time-to-event objective. BTTN, described next, trains readable block-term frequency and lead factors end to end with a survival objective and keeps the clinical factors outside the block-term layer.

\section{Method}

\subsection{Spectral tensor and block-term layer}

Each index ECG is a 10-second, 12-lead recording sampled at 500 Hz \cite{https://doi.org/10.13026/4nqg-sb35}. Each lead was high-pass filtered at 0.5 Hz with a second-order filter and notch filtered at 50 Hz. It was then denoised by soft thresholding of a three-level sym4 wavelet decomposition, decimated to 250 Hz, centred, and scaled by its maximum absolute value. A magnitude spectrogram was computed per lead with a 128-sample window (512 ms) and a 32-sample hop (128 ms). Frequencies up to 40 Hz were kept, magnitudes were transformed by $\log(1+x)$, and the time axis was resampled to 40 frames. Mains interference at 60 Hz, the mains frequency of the source hospital, therefore lies outside the kept band. The result is a tensor $\mathcal{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times T \times L}$ with $F = 21$ frequency bins from 0 to 39 Hz, 1.95 Hz apart, $T = 40$ time frames and $L = 12$ leads, standardised per recording. Within each training fold, each lead was standardised again with the statistics of the training patients. A time shift of a signal changes the phase of its Fourier transform and leaves the magnitude unchanged. A magnitude spectrogram therefore changes little when the beat shifts within a window, so beats need no alignment across recordings.

The block-term layer has $K = 12$ blocks. Block $k$ holds a frequency factor $\mathbf{A}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times 3}$ and a lead factor $\mathbf{C}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times 2}$, and it projects the tensor onto them:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{Z}_k = \mathcal{X} \times_1 \mathbf{A}_k^{\top} \times_3 \mathbf{C}_k^{\top} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times T \times 2},
\label{eq:block}
\end{equation}
where $\times_n$ denotes the mode-$n$ product, which multiplies each mode-$n$ fibre of the tensor by the matrix. A mode-$n$ fibre is the vector obtained by fixing every index of the tensor except the $n$-th. Each block thus gives six time series, one for each pair of a frequency component and a lead component, and the layer gives 72. The projection reduces the frequency and lead modes to ranks 3 and 2, the mode-$n$ ranks by which a block term is characterised \cite{De_Lathauwer_2008}. The time mode is left to the temporal layer of Section~\ref{sec:temporal}. A frequency factor states which frequency bands its block weighs, and a lead factor states which leads. The two can therefore be read as a spectral profile and a spatial profile of the block.

BTTN generalises linear BTTR \cite{Faes_2024}. Both models decompose the input into blocks with one factor per mode. BTTR fits its blocks one at a time by deflation, keeps a static score vector per block and predicts with a linear score. BTTN trains all blocks jointly by gradient descent and replaces the static score vector with learned temporal kernels. It pools their output, passes it through a nonlinear head and is fitted with a Cox survival objective. Figure~\ref{fig:bttr-to-bttn} shows the two models one above the other. The frequency and lead factors keep the same role in both models, so the factors of BTTN can be read as those of BTTR can.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig-bttr-to-bttn.png}
    \caption{Linear block-term tensor regression (BTTR) and the block-term tensor network (BTTN). Top row: BTTR \cite{Faes_2024}, labelled in the image with the online year of that article, splits the input tensor into blocks. Each block has two factor matrices, a core $\mathbf{G}_k$ and a static score vector $\mathbf{t}_k$. The blocks are fitted by deflation and give a linear score. Bottom row: BTTN takes the frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor of one ECG. In each of $K = 12$ blocks it projects the tensor onto a frequency factor $\mathbf{A}_k$ and a lead factor $\mathbf{C}_k$, then filters along time with learned kernels $\Psi_k$ of three widths. In the reported model, the box labelled ``attention over blocks'' pools each channel along time by an attention-weighted sum and by its maximum. A nonlinear head maps the pooled values to a log-risk, and the network is trained end to end with a Cox survival objective. The clinical term is not drawn. It is a linear function of age, sex and ECG intervals, added to the head's log-risk without passing through the block-term layer. The risk curve is schematic; the text states the horizons at which the risk is evaluated.}
    \label{fig:bttr-to-bttn}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Temporal convolution, pooling, head and survival objective}
\label{sec:temporal}

The 72 time series of the block-term layer pass through depthwise temporal convolutions at three scales. Each series is convolved with six kernels of each of the lengths 3, 7 and 15 frames, so that each output channel depends on one series of one block. Batch normalisation and a rectified linear unit follow. Each channel is then pooled along time in two ways: by one set of attention weights over time, computed from all channels by a softmax and shared by them, and by its maximum. The two pooled vectors are concatenated. A head with two hidden layers of 256 and 128 units, batch normalisation, rectified linear units and dropout of 0.4 maps the pooled vector to a scalar log-risk $\eta_{\mathrm{ECG}}$. This network without clinical factors is the ECG term of BTTN, called ECG-only BTTN when it is evaluated alone.

BTTN is trained with the negative Cox partial log-likelihood \cite{Cox_1972}, as DeepSurv trains a neural network \cite{Katzman_2018}. For a mini-batch with event indicators $\delta_i$, times $t_i$ and log-risks $\eta_i$, the loss is
\begin{equation}
\ell = -\frac{1}{\sum_i \delta_i} \sum_i \delta_i \Big( \eta_i - \log \sum_{j:\, t_j \ge t_i} e^{\eta_j} \Big),
\label{eq:cox}
\end{equation}
with risk sets formed within the batch. The time is the interval from the index ECG to AF onset for cases and to the end of follow-up for controls. Training used Adam with a learning rate of $2 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay of $10^{-4}$, a cosine schedule over at most 120 epochs and batches of 512. Batches without an event were skipped. Within each training fold, 15\% of the patients were held out for early stopping on the validation AUROC, with a patience of 16 epochs. The prediction for a test fold is the mean log-risk of three networks trained with different seeds.

\subsection{Clinical term and readable factors}

The clinical term is a linear function of nine risk factors. They are age, sex, and seven automated measurements of the index ECG from MIMIC-IV-ECG. The RR interval and the P, QRS and T axes are used as released. The P-wave duration, QRS duration and QT interval were computed from the released onset and end times of the waves. Age is the anchor age of MIMIC-IV, the age of the patient in a shifted anchor year that the database assigns to each patient \cite{Johnson_2023}. The factors are standardised with the statistics of the training fold, and a missing value is replaced by the training mean. The term $\mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{c}$ has no intercept and is added to the log-risk of the ECG term:
\begin{equation}
\eta = \eta_{\mathrm{ECG}}(\mathcal{X}) + \mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{c}.
\label{eq:fused}
\end{equation}
The weights $\mathbf{w}$ are initialised from a class-balanced logistic regression on the training split. They are excluded from weight decay, so that training does not shrink them towards zero. The PR interval is not among the factors. We call BTTN with the clinical term the fused model.

This construction makes the factors readable by design and keeps the clinical term out of them. In this paper, readable names this property of the factors of a model that is interpretable by design. It is the sense in which the title calls BTTN interpretable. Every path from the ECG to the risk passes through the block projections of Equation~\eqref{eq:block}. The frequency and lead factors are therefore parameters on which the prediction depends, and they can be read directly. The clinical term reaches the log-risk without passing through the block-term layer. Adding it leaves the role of the factors unchanged, and the risk factors can be read as a linear term beside the ECG evidence. Readability is here a property of the architecture, and this study reports no reading of the learned frequency and lead factors.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Cohort and labels}
\label{sec:cohort}

The cohort comes from MIMIC-IV-ECG, about 800,000 diagnostic ECGs from nearly 160,000 patients \cite{https://doi.org/10.13026/4nqg-sb35}. It is linked to the hospital module of MIMIC-IV, drawn from the electronic health record of the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center \cite{Johnson_2023}. Incident AF is the first hospital admission with a diagnosis of AF or flutter (ICD-10 I48, or ICD-9 427.31 or 427.32 for older admissions), dated by its admission time. We counted AF and flutter together, since a coded flutter diagnosis did not reliably separate flutter from AF in a registry validation \cite{Rix_2012}. We preferred hospital diagnoses to labels read from the ECG, since only 65.4\% of 700 AF-labelled ECGs in MIMIC-IV showed AF on expert review \cite{Valasakis_2026}.

The index ECG is each patient's earliest ECG whose machine report reads sinus rhythm, without AF, flutter, or a paced, junctional or ectopic atrial rhythm. A patient was excluded as prevalent or concurrent when an AF diagnosis, or an ECG read as AF, fell before the end of a 30-day blanking window after the index ECG. Follow-up ends at the later of the last ECG and death. In the one-year design, cases had onset more than 30 and at most 365 days after the index ECG. Controls had no AF diagnosis and at least 365 days of follow-up. The survival design kept onset at any time after the blanking window, with the same controls. For modelling, the one-year design used all 1,855 cases and four controls per case, drawn from 16,000 sampled controls, for 9,275 patients by design. The survival design used 4,000 sampled events and 16,000 sampled controls, 20,000 patients in all.

\subsection{Baselines, protocol and metrics}

Three baselines shared the cohort, folds and inputs of BTTN. The clinical model is a class-balanced logistic regression on the nine factors of the clinical term, with median imputation and standardisation. Linear BTTR \cite{Faes_2024} was fitted on the same spectral tensor to the label coded as $-1$ and $+1$, with up to eight blocks. The CNN applies three one-dimensional convolutional layers of 32, 64 and 64 channels along time, with frequency and lead flattened into input channels. With batch normalisation, dropout and global average pooling, it was trained with weighted cross-entropy and early stopping as a three-member ensemble. In the survival design, the clinical baseline is a Cox model with a ridge penalty of 0.1 on the same factors.

The one-year comparison used stratified five-fold cross-validation at the patient level, repeated over five seeds, with each patient's scores averaged over seeds. The survival model used one stratified five-fold split, with an event within one year as the label for early stopping. Each patient's risk in the survival design came from the fold in which that patient was held out. The clinical Cox model was fitted on the same training folds as the networks. AUROC intervals come from 2,000 bootstrap resamples with percentile limits. At each horizon, cases had an event by the horizon and controls were AF-free beyond it, and patients censored earlier were excluded.

Calibration and clinical use were assessed at one year in the survival sample, after two-fold cross-fitted logistic recalibration. Cross-fitting splits the sample into two halves, fits the recalibration on one half, applies it to the other, and then swaps the halves. Calibration and clinical use were measured by Brier scores, decision curves \cite{Vickers_2006} and operating points at the top 5, 10, 20 and 30\% of risk. A decision curve plots net benefit against the threshold probability at which a patient would be treated. Net benefit is the proportion of true positives minus the proportion of false positives, weighted by the odds of the threshold probability \cite{Vickers_2006}. Positive predictive value (PPV) and the number needed to monitor, its inverse, were recomputed at the proportion of cases among the cases and AF-free controls of the one-year design, 1,855 in 43,625. We report the study with reference to the TRIPOD+AI statement \cite{Collins_2024}, without a table of participant characteristics and without counts of missing values per clinical factor.

\subsection{Occlusion readout}

Occlusion sensitivity covers part of the input and records the change in the output \cite{Zeiler_2014}. It is one of the perturbation-based explanation methods \cite{Ivanovs_2021}. We applied it to a single ECG-only BTTN trained on the whole survival sample. For the 120 highest-risk ECGs of patients with an event, a window of 12 samples (24 ms at 500 Hz) was set to zero in every beat of one lead. The window ran from 6 samples before to 5 samples after a fixed offset from each R peak. Each offset was rounded to a whole sample towards the R peak. R peaks were detected on lead II. The spectrogram was rebuilt and the drop in log-risk recorded, for each lead and for offsets from 350 ms before to 425 ms after the R peak in steps of 25 ms. Neighbouring windows abut or leave one sample between them. Zeroing a high-energy part of the beat changes the spectrogram most. Each mean drop was therefore also divided by the sum of two terms: the mean energy of the removed samples, and, for stability, one tenth of that energy's mean over all leads and offsets.

The offsets from 225 ms before to 375 ms after the R peak were grouped into five windows, the P, PR, QRS, ST and T windows. Table~\ref{tab:occlusion} gives the offsets each window contains and the span of the beat that their occlusions cover. The offsets from 350 to 250 ms before the R peak and at 400 and 425 ms after it belong to no window. They are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:beat-saliency}, and their removed energy counts in the mean energy over all leads and offsets that the normalisation adds. They enter no window value. These windows are fixed offsets and are not delineated per beat. A negative mean drop at a lead and offset was set to zero before the window means were taken, for both the raw and the normalised value. The profile of a window is the mean over leads and over the offsets it contains. The zero replacement and the 24 ms window are choices of this readout, and the limitations discuss how such choices shape the profile. The readout describes the fitted model on its own training ECGs.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Study cohort}

Of 151,941 patients with an eligible sinus-rhythm index ECG, 1,855 developed incident AF within one year and 41,770 qualified as controls (Table~\ref{tab:cohort}). The survival design held 8,775 events with the same 41,770 controls. The exclusion categories were counted over all eligible patients and overlap. The table therefore lists each count against the eligible total. Incident events numbered 1,855, 2,828 and 5,262 by one, two and five years. Controls had a median follow-up of 1,601 days (interquartile range 880 to 2,559). Of the controls, 40,960 (98.1\%) had an ECG after the index ECG.

