\subsection{Discrimination at one year and across horizons}

In the one-year design, the fused model had the highest AUROC of the methods compared, 0.7776 (95\% CI [0.7664, 0.7888]) (Table~\ref{tab:oneyear}). Its interval lay above the CNN's interval of [0.7226, 0.7469]. It also lay above the interval of ECG-only BTTN, 0.7397 [0.7275, 0.7517], so the fused model discriminated better than ECG-only BTTN. The fused model's interval overlapped the interval of the clinical model, 0.7597 [0.7488, 0.7713]. The study reports no paired interval for the difference between the fused and clinical models, so we report their estimates side by side. The CNN reached 0.7350, with an interval that overlapped that of ECG-only BTTN, so the two are comparable on this cohort. ECG-only BTTN exceeded linear BTTR, at 0.5967 [0.5824, 0.6115], with intervals that do not overlap.

