In the survival design, the fused model had point estimates above those of the clinical Cox model at each horizon (Table~\ref{tab:horizons}). The same cross-validated risks were read at every horizon without refitting. The fused model had an AUROC of 0.723 [0.701, 0.745] at 120 days, for onset in the 90 days after the blanking window, 31 to 120 days after the index ECG. Its AUROC was 0.727 [0.711, 0.743] at one year, 0.740 [0.727, 0.753] at two years and 0.745 [0.735, 0.755] at five years. The clinical Cox model had point estimates of 0.710, 0.711, 0.727 and 0.731. ECG-only BTTN ranged from 0.658 to 0.676. The four horizons share the same risks and overlapping patients. They describe the behaviour of each model and are not independent replications. One reason for the lower one-year values of the survival design is its control group. At one year it includes patients who develop AF later, whereas the controls of the one-year design have no AF diagnosis.

