\subsection{Calibration, risk stratification and operating points}

After logistic recalibration within the survival sample, the largest gap between the predicted and observed one-year proportions of the fused model lay in the sixth decile, among 19,986 patients with 838 cases (Figure~\ref{fig:clinical-utility}). In that decile (bar 5 of Figure~\ref{fig:clinical-utility}), 4.95\% was observed against a mean prediction of 3.56\%. The deciles are read in the survival sample, which holds a larger share of the events of the cohort than of its controls (4,000 of 8,775 against 16,000 of 41,770). Its proportions of AF are therefore higher than those of the cohort. Observed one-year AF rose from 0.55\% in the lowest decile to 11.4\% in the highest, with mean predicted risks of 0.61\% and 12.3\%. The sixth decile also had a higher observed proportion than the seventh (bar 6, 4.15\%). Brier scores, the mean squared difference between the predicted probability and the outcome, were 0.0390 for the fused model, 0.0396 for ECG-only BTTN and 0.0398 for the clinical model. The fused model thus had the lowest Brier score of the three, although the three scores differ only in the fourth decimal place.

Sensitivity and PPV were higher for the fused model than for the clinical model at all four flagged fractions (Table~\ref{tab:operating}). At the top 10\% of risk, the fused model had a sensitivity of 0.272 against 0.240 for the clinical model, at specificities of 0.908 and 0.906. At the proportion of cases in the one-year design, its PPV was 0.1155 against 0.1019, a number needed to monitor of 8.7 against 9.8. In the decision curves of Figure~\ref{fig:clinical-utility}, the curve of the fused model lay above the other two between threshold probabilities of about 0.03 and 0.12. All three models had a net benefit above both treating all and treating none at threshold probabilities from about 0.04 to about 0.1. Below about 0.04, their curves lay close to that of treating all, and all curves lay near zero above about 0.12.

