\subsection{Cardiac-cycle occlusion profile}

Per unit of energy removed, the occlusion profile of the ECG term was highest in the PR window, at 0.00216 (Table~\ref{tab:occlusion}). The ST window (0.00157), the P window (0.00152) and the T window (0.00127) followed, and the QRS window was lowest (0.00086). The P and ST windows were close to each other. Raw occlusion was instead dominated by the QRS window, where the mean drop in log-risk was 0.0232 against 0.0037 in the PR window. The QRS complex has the highest amplitude of the beat, so zeroing it removes the most energy and changes the spectrogram most. Figure~\ref{fig:beat-saliency} shows the energy-normalised response on the mean median beat of each lead. The profile describes what the fitted ECG term responds to on 120 high-risk training ECGs.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig-beat-saliency.png}
