\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Comparison with published incident-AF models}

Published results give context for the discrimination of the fused model within one health system. On the hospital behind MIMIC-IV, a survival-loss CNN reached a C-index of 0.750 \cite{Sau_2026} and an image-based model 0.754 \cite{Zeidaabadi_2026}. A Montreal model reached an AUROC of 0.77 on external validation in MIMIC-IV, with a label source that its abstract does not state \cite{Jabbour_2024}. Elsewhere, an AUROC of 0.85 at one year \cite{Raghunath_2021} and a C-index of 0.781 over a short follow-up \cite{Riek_2026} were reported. These studies differ from the present one in horizon, outcome definition, sample and metric. A systematic review shows how far such choices move reported values \cite{Tsiartas_2025}. Identification studies \cite{Attia_2019,Yuan_2023} address another task. So does AF diagnosis from the ECG. A foundation model reported an AUROC of 0.996 for the AF label of multi-label ECG interpretation \cite{McKeen_2025}. Its authors also released an open benchmark on MIMIC-IV-ECG \cite{McKeen_2025}.

The gain of the fused model over the clinical model agrees with reports that ECG and clinical information are complementary \cite{Khurshid_2022,Christopoulos_2020}. The operating point of the fused model at the top 10\% of risk is of the same order as that of one externally validated model. That model flagged 9.7\% of older patients with a sensitivity of 31\% and a specificity of 92\%, in a different population with chart-confirmed endpoints \cite{Pfeifer_2026}.

The comparability of ECG-only BTTN with the CNN bears on the reported accuracy cost of intrinsic interpretability on the ECG \cite{Patlatzoglou_2025}. In this design, ECG-only BTTN, built on readable factors, reached the discrimination of a compact CNN. The CNN has three convolutional layers, so the result says nothing about parity with the large networks of published work.

\subsection{Implications of an ECG term built on readable factors, with a separate clinical term}

Separating the clinical term from the block-term layer lets the fused model gain discrimination without passing the risk factors through its ECG factors. The ECG evidence still passes through frequency and lead factors, and each risk factor enters the linear term with one weight. The two sources therefore have separate parameters that can be inspected apart. The fused model is trained as one network, so its factors are fitted beside the clinical term and may differ from those of ECG-only BTTN. In a fused network whose clinical inputs enter the block-term layer, the factors could absorb risk-factor information, as concept representations absorb information beyond their concepts \cite{arxiv_2106_13314}. The architecture of BTTN closes this route for the clinical term. It does not close it for information that the ECG itself carries, which the factors may encode in ways a reader does not expect.

The PR-window concentration of the occlusion profile is in line with saliency reports of other incident-AF models. These pointed to the P-wave \cite{Khurshid_2022} and to P-wave morphology and the PR interval \cite{Zeidaabadi_2026}. It is also in line with cohort studies that tie the PR interval measured in sinus rhythm to later AF. In Framingham, the PR interval was a predictor in the AF risk score \cite{Schnabel_2009}, and first-degree atrioventricular block carried an adjusted hazard ratio of 2.06 \cite{Cheng_2009}. Each standard deviation of the PR interval carried a hazard ratio of 1.13 in older Black and White adults \cite{Magnani_2013}. First-degree block carried a hazard ratio of 1.75 in an older Japanese population \cite{Tanaka_2021}. In Black adults of the Jackson Heart Study, the PR interval, P-wave duration and P-wave terminal force were each associated with AF \cite{Yogeswaran_2025}. P-wave indices show the same direction in a two-cohort study \cite{Magnani_2015} and in a meta-analysis whose evidence was of very low certainty in selected populations \cite{Emmanuela_2026}.

The agreement with the cohort studies is partial in two respects. First, in a Swedish cohort of 70-year-olds, the PR interval measured in lead V1 was not related to incident AF, while a short P-wave duration was \cite{Ostrowska_2022}. Second, the cohort studies measure the PR interval from the onset of the P wave to the onset of the QRS complex. The PR window of the readout instead groups the occlusions centred 125 to 75 ms before the R peak, a fixed span from 136 to 62 ms before it. On the mean beat of Figure~\ref{fig:beat-saliency}, this span holds the end of the P wave and the PR segment. The agreement claimed here therefore concerns the location within the cardiac cycle only.

Two readings of these results concern the atria, and we offer both as hypotheses. The clinical term holds P-wave duration but no PR interval. One reading of the gain of the fused model is that the ECG term carries information on atrial and atrioventricular conduction that the clinical term does not measure. The second reading rests on a clinical frame for an atrial focus. An expert consensus defines atrial cardiomyopathy as structural, architectural, contractile or electrophysiological changes of the atria that can produce clinically relevant manifestations, among them arrhythmia \cite{Goette_2016}. In a Japanese community cohort, prolonged P-wave duration was associated both with incident AF and with more left atrial fibrosis at autopsy \cite{Maezono_2025}. In this reading, the PR-window response reflects such atrial change. The present results test neither reading. Testing the second needs studies that pair the ECG with measurements of the atrium.

\subsection{Limitations}

The study used one database from one health system and has no external validation in a cohort with dated outcomes. Its cohort is also selected by follow-up. Controls needed at least 365 days of follow-up, and 88,945 of the 151,941 eligible patients had no AF and follow-up under 365 days (Table~\ref{tab:cohort}). Patients with a year of follow-up in this health system may differ from those without, so this requirement may bias the estimates of discrimination and calibration. The study also reports no table of the characteristics of cases and controls and no count of missing values per clinical factor. The sample therefore cannot be compared with another population on these measures.

The labels of the study are ICD-coded hospital diagnoses, which are mostly correct but incomplete. A meta-analysis of 24 validation studies pooled a PPV of 88\% and a sensitivity of 80\% for administrative AF definitions \cite{Yao_2019}. In a population study, a hospital discharge diagnosis found 73.7\% of record-verified AF, with a PPV of 88.5\% \cite{Malmo_2016}. A registry found a PPV of 92.6\% for the combined AF or flutter diagnosis \cite{Rix_2012}. Some controls may therefore have AF diagnosed only outside hospital. Onset is dated by the admission and not by the first occurrence of AF. Follow-up to death counts patients without a hospital AF diagnosis as AF-free.

The clinical model is narrower and less exact than a full clinical score. Age is the anchor age of MIMIC-IV, which is not the age at the index ECG \cite{Johnson_2023}. The clinical factors omit variables of the Framingham score, among them systolic blood pressure, hypertension treatment, body mass index and heart failure \cite{Schnabel_2009}. They also omit the PR interval, a known AF predictor \cite{Cheng_2009,Magnani_2013}. The CNN baseline is compact, and a larger network might reach a different value.

The calibration and the PPVs hold for the samples in which they were estimated. The probabilities were recalibrated in the evaluation sample with two-fold cross-fitting, so the calibration of the model in another population is not shown. The one-year design sampled four controls per case, and the survival design sampled cases and controls. PPV was therefore recomputed at the proportion of cases among the cases and AF-free controls of the one-year design, 1,855 in 43,625. This proportion exceeds the one-year rate of the survival cohort, 1,855 in 50,545, whose one-year negatives include the patients with onset after one year. The PPVs of Table~\ref{tab:operating} are therefore upper values for that population.

The occlusion readout is a perturbation explanation of the fitted model on 120 high-risk training ECGs. It is open to the critiques of post-hoc attribution \cite{arxiv_1810_03292,arxiv_1806_10758,Arends_2026}. Its zero replacement, its 24 ms window and its fixed windows relative to the R peak shape the profile, since the replacement value and the window size shape an occlusion map \cite{arxiv_1702_04595}. The perturbation method and the region size likewise shape the evaluation of attributions on time series \cite{_imi__2025}. Another replacement could change the order of the windows. The energy normalisation may also shape that order. Division by the removed energy favours windows of low energy, so the PR window may rank first in part because occluding it removes little energy.

Readable factors do not by themselves show that the nonlinear head routes its decision through them in the way their reading suggests \cite{arxiv_2106_13314}. The readability of BTTN rests on its architecture, and this study reports no measurement of how far the factors carry the decision on ICD-coded labels.

\subsection{Future research}

These studies would extend this work:
\begin{itemize}
\item A test of the block factors on ICD-coded labels would measure how far the factors carry the decision. It would use control probes and the removal of top-ranked against bottom-ranked factors, in the manner of remove-and-retrain evaluation \cite{arxiv_1806_10758}.
\item External validation in a cohort with dated outcomes, and temporal validation within MIMIC-IV, would test whether the model transports, that is, whether its discrimination and calibration hold in a population or period other than the one it was developed in. Such validation would be reported with reference to the TRIPOD+AI statement \cite{Collins_2024}.
\item A clinical model that includes the PR interval and the variables of a full clinical score would sharpen the comparison with the clinical model of this study.
\item A paired interval for the difference between the fused and the clinical model would show whether the one-year gain exceeds sampling variation.
\item An occlusion readout on held-out ECGs, with replacements other than zero, would show how far the PR-window profile depends on the choices of the present readout.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

On ICD-coded hospital diagnoses linked to MIMIC-IV-ECG, the fused model, a block-term tensor network with a separate clinical term, had the highest one-year discrimination of the methods compared. Its AUROC was 0.7776, against 0.7597 for the clinical model and 0.7350 for a CNN. Without refitting for each horizon, it kept its point estimates above those of the clinical model at horizons from 120 days to five years after the index ECG. After logistic recalibration within the survival sample, its mean predicted one-year risk was 0.61\% in the lowest risk decile and 12.3\% in the highest, against observed proportions of 0.55\% and 11.4\%. The largest gap, in the sixth decile, was 4.95\% observed against 3.56\% predicted. The fused model also flagged more future cases than the clinical model at each flagged fraction. Its gain over the clinical model is small, and the one-year intervals overlap. The gain is consistent in direction within one health system, and no interval for the difference yet establishes it.

ECG-only BTTN reached the discrimination of a compact CNN under the same protocol, while its evidence passes through frequency and lead factors that lie on the predictive path and can be read. The clinical term adds the risk factors without passing through those factors. Per unit of signal energy, the ECG term responded most to the PR window of the beat. This is the part of the cardiac cycle that cohort studies of the PR interval tie to later AF \cite{Cheng_2009,Magnani_2013}. The readout describes the fitted model, and its atrial reading remains a hypothesis.

For the design of ECG risk models, the results suggest that a clinical term kept outside the block-term factors adds to the discrimination of the ECG term. At one year, the fused model reached 0.7776 [0.7664, 0.7888] and ECG-only BTTN 0.7397 [0.7275, 0.7517]. These two intervals do not overlap, although the study reports no paired interval for this difference. The clinical model used here is narrower than a full clinical score, and the readout is descriptive. Validation outside one health system, a test of the factors on ICD-coded labels and a clinical model that includes the PR interval are the next requirements before any clinical use.

